  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1220 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nEntering the weekend, the main weather story is that rain is very\nlikely across a majority of the forecast area, but rain amounts \nwon't be too impressive for most locations. It is also looking \nlike temperatures will be even cooler than anticipated thanks to \nshowers and dense cloud cover, keeping high temperatures in the \n40s to mid 50s throughout the weekend. Cooler and cloudy \nconditions persist early next week as our upper level pattern \ncalms down, but consistently breezy south winds should stick \naround as well. Our next active stretch of weather is favored to \narrive mid-late next week, as models have been fairly consistent \npredicting a stronger system for the region that would provide \nadditional rain opportunities.\n\nA lead shortwave will pass just south of the Panhandles ahead of a \ntrailing closed low pressure system early Saturday, spurring on \nbreezier south winds which should advect better moisture \nnorthward. Weak forcing form the lead shortwave should then begin\nproducing light showers across the western and central Panhandles\nearly Saturday morning-afternoon (30-60% POPs). The far more \npromising chance for rain should then arrive with the main low \npressure system models show approaching from our northwest late \nSaturday into Sunday. Rain chances have further amplified during \nthe Saturday night into Sunday time frame, now greater than 70% \nacross much of the Panhandles. By Sunday afternoon-evening, the \nsystem will begin to exit the region, with a dry slot filtering in\nand a weak cold front sagging southward. Most if not all rain \nshould be over by Sunday night, although any lingering wrap around\nmoisture could generate a few more weak showers (20-30% POPs).\n\nAs previously alluded to, any rain earlier Saturday would likely be \non the lighter side, as limited forcing and PWATs of 0.5-0.8\" are \ngood but not great (especially with little to no CAPE). Through the \nbulk of the event, rounds of light to moderate rain showers are \nexpected across the area. Locally heavier pockets of rain could be \nachieved over the east and possibly even central Panhandles where \nPWATs will be closer to 1\", leading to 40-70% chances for rainfall \nto exceed 0.5\". Occasional weak instability may allow some anemic \nthunderstorms to mix in as the core of the low passes overhead, but \nrain rates still don't look overly impressive at any point. The way \nthings are trending, most of the CWA would receive only a quarter to \nhalf inch of rain or less, with biggest winners getting 0.5-1.0\".\n\nHarrel",
